<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Forward-mode automatic differentiation (<span id="abstract1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">FmAD</span>) and zero-order (ZO) optimization have been proposed as memory-efficient alternatives to backpropagation (BP) for gradient computation, especially in low-resource settings.
However, their practical benefits remain unclear due to two key gaps:
a lack of comparison against memory-efficient BP variants, such as activation checkpointing, and a lack of a unified theoretical analysis.
This work presents a comprehensive theoretical and empirical comparison of BP, <span id="abstract1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">FmAD</span>, and ZO methods.
Our theoretical analysis shows that while <span id="abstract1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">FmAD</span>, and ZO can reduce memory usage, they incur significant costs in accuracy, convergence speed, and computation compared to BP with checkpointing.
These drawbacks worsen with larger models or constrained perturbation budgets.
Empirical experiments on large language and vision-language models show that BP with checkpointing outperforms <span id="abstract1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">FmAD</span> and ZO variants, including those enhanced with variance reduction, achieving up to 31.1% higher accuracy, 34.8% faster convergence, and 3.8 <math id="abstract1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math> fewer computations at comparable memory usage.
Our results highlight fundamental limitations of <span id="abstract1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">FmAD</span> and ZO, and reaffirm BP with checkpointing as the most effective strategy for model training under memory-constrained settings.
Our code is available at  <a href="https://github.com/Astuary/The_Cost_of_Avoiding_Backpropagation" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://github.com/Astuary/The_Cost_of_Avoiding_Backpropagation</a>.</p>
  
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Backpropagation (BP) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite> is the standard algorithm for gradient computation in deep learning due to its convergence efficiency and widespread support in automatic differentiation frameworks such as PyTorch <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite> and JAX <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>.
However, BP incurs high memory overhead in training large models, as it must store intermediate activations for the backward pass.
To address this limitation, recent research has explored alternative gradient estimation methods such as forward-mode automatic differentiation (<span id="S1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">FmAD</span>) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>, <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>, <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite> and zero-order (ZO) optimization <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>, <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>, which approximate gradients – using directional derivatives or two forward pass evaluations – based on randomly perturbed weights.
These methods are often promoted as memory-efficient or hardware-friendly alternatives to BP, especially in resource-constrained or non-differentiable settings.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Despite growing interest, prior work on <span id="S1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">FmAD</span> and ZO suffers from two critical limitations that leave their practical value inadequately understood.
First, existing comparisons often overlook activation checkpointing <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite>, a widely supported and effective BP variant that substantially reduces memory usage by recomputing rather than storing intermediate activations.
Without including BP with checkpointing as a baseline, claims of memory efficiency from <span id="S1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">FmAD</span> and ZO are incomplete and potentially misleading.
Second, there is a lack of unified theoretical grounding of these alternatives.
Key aspects, such as convergence behavior, computational complexity, and memory trade-offs, remain poorly characterized.
Without a unified theoretical comparison, it remains unclear under what conditions, if any, these methods offer advantages over standard or BP with checkpointing.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">This paper addresses both limitations through comprehensive theoretical and empirical studies of the BP, <span id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">FmAD</span>, and ZO approaches. We analyze their convergence behavior, memory consumption, and computational cost as functions of model dimensionality <math id="S1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> and the number of perturbations per iteration <math id="S1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>.
Our theoretical analysis reveals that, while <span id="S1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">FmAD</span> and ZO can reduce memory under certain regimes (e.g., when perturbations are evaluated sequentially), they fundamentally suffer from poor scalability.
Specifically, both methods incur higher per-iteration computational cost, <math id="S1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(nd)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(nd)</annotation></semantics></math>, and their convergence rates degrade with increasing dimensionality or decreasing perturbation budget due to stricter learning rate constraints.
By contrast, BP with activation checkpointing, achieves optimal first-order convergence with lower computational cost and comparable memory usage.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S1.F1" class="ltx_figure ltx_align_floatright" style="width:50%;"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2506.21833v1/three_way_tradeoff.svg" id="S1.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:238/146;" width="238" height="146"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S1.F1.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 1</span>: </span><span id="S1.F1.7" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">
The three-way trade-off between accuracy, convergence time, and memory consumption during training of <span id="S1.F1.7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Llama 3.1</span> (8B) on the AGNews dataset.
The circle radii are proportional to the memory consumption.
<span id="S1.F1.7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">BP-Checkpointing</span> achieves highest accuracy with lowest convergence time using comparable memory to <span id="S1.F1.7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">FmAD</span> and ZO variants.
§<a href="#S4" title="4 Empirical Evaluation ‣ The Cost of Avoiding Backpropagation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> describes these methods in detail.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">To complement our theoretical findings, we conduct extensive empirical evaluations on large language and vision-language models across tasks including text classification, text generation, and visual question answering.
We compare BP with checkpointing against a wide range of <span id="S1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">FmAD</span> and ZO variants (including
SVRG <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite>,
multiple perturbations per iteration <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>),
and our own enhanced versions with variance reduction: gradient accumulation and adaptive perturbation sampling.
As illustrated in Figure <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ The Cost of Avoiding Backpropagation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, BP with checkpointing consistently achieves higher accuracy and faster convergence, while using memory on par with <span id="S1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">FmAD</span> and ZO variants.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">These findings lead to a critical insight: despite recent enthusiasm for forward-mode and zero-order methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>, <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>, <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>, <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite>, they remain fundamentally constrained by their inability to efficiently scale to large models.
Rather than serving as alternatives to backpropagation, they operate as inefficient approximations that trade off accuracy or convergence speed for marginal memory reductions.
Our analysis reaffirms BP with checkpointing as the most effective and scalable strategy for training modern deep neural networks under memory-constrained settings.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">This paper’s main contributions are:</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize" style="--ltx-enum-leftmargin:0.35cm;">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A unified theoretical analysis and comparison of the convergence rates, memory cost, and compute complexity of BP, <span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">FmAD</span>, and ZO.
We show that <span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">FmAD</span> and ZO require smaller learning rates when
<math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> is large or
<math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> is small, leading to slower convergence and higher per-iteration compute cost.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A comprehensive empirical study on large-scale models across diverse tasks, showing that <span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">BP</span> with checkpointing achieves 4.5–31.1% higher accuracy, 21.4–34.8% faster convergence, and 3.2–3.8<math id="S1.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math> lower computation cost than <span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">FmAD</span> and ZO variants, while maintaining comparable memory usage.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The design and benchmarking of two new variance reduction methods for <span id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">FmAD</span> and ZO.
These methods improve accuracy by 7.5–14.0%, but still fall short of BP’s overall efficiency, and introduce overheads in either convergence time or memory.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">An analysis of <span id="S1.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">FmAD</span>’s and ZO’s failure modes (Appendix <a href="#A6.SS5" title="F.5 Failure Mode Analysis ‣ Appendix F Additional Results ‣ The Cost of Avoiding Backpropagation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">F.5</span></a>), identifying how high-dimensional perturbations and noisy Jacobian-vector products contribute to instability and poor convergence.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Background</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This section reviews three gradient computation techniques central to our study:
(a) reverse-mode automatic differentiation (<span id="S2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">RmAD</span>, of which backpropagation is a special case),
(b) forward-mode automatic differentiation (<span id="S2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">FmAD</span>), and
(c) zero-order (ZO) finite-difference methods.
For an in-depth survey of these approaches, we refer readers to <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>.
Appendix <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Related Work ‣ The Cost of Avoiding Backpropagation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a> reviews related work in detail.
Details on signal propagation mechanism of these methods are in Appendix <a href="#A7" title="Appendix G Signal Propagation for Gradient Computation ‣ The Cost of Avoiding Backpropagation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">G</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The three methods described below operate on a function <math id="S2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>, which in deep learning corresponds to a neural network and can be non-convex.
This function <math id="S2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> is composed of nested functions <math id="S2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in[p]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in[p]</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> is the number of layers given a neural network. Each nested function produces intermediate activations <math id="S2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{i}=f_{i}(w_{i},y_{i-1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{i}=f_{i}(w_{i},y_{i-1})</annotation></semantics></math>, given weights <math id="S2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and previous activations <math id="S2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{i-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{i-1}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{0}=x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{0}=x</annotation></semantics></math> is the input.
The weights are represented by the vector <math id="S2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{w}={w_{1},w_{2},\dots,w_{p}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝒘</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{w}={w_{1},w_{2},\dots,w_{p}}</annotation></semantics></math>, where each <math id="S2.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{[1,\dots,p]}\in\mathbb{R}^{[m_{1},\dots,m_{p}]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{[1,\dots,p]}\in\mathbb{R}^{[m_{1},\dots,m_{p}]}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The total number of trainable parameter is <math id="S2.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=\sum_{i=1}^{p}m_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>p</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=\sum_{i=1}^{p}m_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The intermediate activations are <math id="S2.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{y}={y_{1},\dots,y_{p}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{y}={y_{1},\dots,y_{p}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The final output is <math id="S2.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y=y_{p}=f(\boldsymbol{w},x)\in\mathbb{R}^{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒘</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>q</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y=y_{p}=f(\boldsymbol{w},x)\in\mathbb{R}^{q}</annotation></semantics></math>, where typically <math id="S2.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q\ll m_{i},\forall i\in[p]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>≪</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q\ll m_{i},\forall i\in[p]</annotation></semantics></math>.
The loss function <math id="S2.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}(y,\hat{y})\in\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}(y,\hat{y})\in\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> measures the difference between the predicted output <math id="S2.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> and the true target values <math id="S2.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{y}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Reverse-mode Auto Differentiation (<span id="S2.p3.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">RmAD</span>).</span>
<span id="S2.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">RmAD</span> computes gradients by propagating sensitivities
(which is the rate at which the output of a function changes with respect to a given intermediate value)
backward through the neural network.
<span id="S2.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">RmAD</span> relies on vector-Jacobian product (<span id="S2.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">vjp</span>), where the <em id="S2.p3.1.5" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Jacobian</em> represents partial derivatives of an intermediate activation <math id="S2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> with respect to weights <math id="S2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{i-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{i-1}</annotation></semantics></math>, denoted <math id="S2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{\partial y_{i}}{\partial w_{i-1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\partial y_{i}}{\partial w_{i-1}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the <em id="S2.p3.1.6" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">vector</em> is the activation gradient <math id="S2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{\partial f}{\partial y_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\partial f}{\partial y_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
<span id="S2.p3.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">RmAD</span> starts by setting <math id="S2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{\partial f}{\partial y_{p}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\partial f}{\partial y_{p}}</annotation></semantics></math> to 1, and propagating <math id="S2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{\partial f}{\partial w_{i-1}}={\frac{\partial f}{\partial y_{i}}}\frac{\partial y_{i}}{\partial w_{i-1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\partial f}{\partial w_{i-1}}={\frac{\partial f}{\partial y_{i}}}\frac{\partial y_{i}}{\partial w_{i-1}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{\partial f}{\partial y_{i-1}}=\frac{\partial f}{\partial y_{i}}\frac{\partial y_{i}}{\partial y_{i-1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\partial f}{\partial y_{i-1}}=\frac{\partial f}{\partial y_{i}}\frac{\partial y_{i}}{\partial y_{i-1}}</annotation></semantics></math>, for <math id="S2.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in[2,p]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in[2,p]</annotation></semantics></math>, backwards.
The final result is the weight gradient <math id="S2.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{\partial f}{\partial\boldsymbol{w}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>𝒘</mi></mrow></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\partial f}{\partial\boldsymbol{w}}</annotation></semantics></math>, formed from a series of <span id="S2.p3.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">vjp</span> computations.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Backpropagation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite> (BP) is a specific case of <span id="S2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">RmAD</span> tailored for neural networks.
While <span id="S2.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">RmAD</span>’s backward pass begins by <math id="S2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{\partial f}{\partial y_{p}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\partial f}{\partial y_{p}}</annotation></semantics></math> set to 1, BP initializes from the gradient of the loss function: <math id="S2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{\partial\mathcal{L}}{\partial y_{p}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\partial\mathcal{L}}{\partial y_{p}}</annotation></semantics></math>, which provides a semantically meaningful signal for optimization.
The backward phase is preceded by a forward pass that computes the activations and the loss <math id="S2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Forward-mode Auto Differentiation (<span id="S2.p5.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">FmAD</span>).</span>
<span id="S2.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">FmAD</span> propagates directional derivatives through the neural network to compute Jacobian-vector products (<span id="S2.p5.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">jvp</span>).
<span id="S2.p5.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">FmAD</span> analyzes how a small perturbation <math id="S2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒗</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{v}</annotation></semantics></math> in the weights <math id="S2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒘</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{w}</annotation></semantics></math> affects the outputs.
Starting from <math id="S2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta y_{1}=\frac{\partial y_{1}}{\partial w_{1}}v_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta y_{1}=\frac{\partial y_{1}}{\partial w_{1}}v_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, <span id="S2.p5.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">FmAD</span> propagates changes forward as:</p>
<table id="S2.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\delta y_{i}=\frac{\partial y_{i}}{\partial w_{i}}v_{i}+\frac{\partial y_{i}}{\partial y_{i-1}}\delta y_{i-1},\;\;\text{for }i\in[2,p]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">y</mi><mi mathsize="0.800em">i</mi></msub></mrow><mo mathsize="0.800em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mo mathsize="0.800em">∂</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">y</mi><mi mathsize="0.800em">i</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mo mathsize="0.800em">∂</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">w</mi><mi mathsize="0.800em">i</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">v</mi><mi mathsize="0.800em">i</mi></msub></mrow><mo mathsize="0.800em">+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mo mathsize="0.800em">∂</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">y</mi><mi mathsize="0.800em">i</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mo mathsize="0.800em">∂</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">y</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">i</mi><mo mathsize="0.800em">−</mo><mn mathsize="0.800em">1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.800em">δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">y</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">i</mi><mo mathsize="0.800em">−</mo><mn mathsize="0.800em">1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo mathsize="0.800em" rspace="0.727em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext mathsize="0.800em">for </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.800em">i</mi></mrow><mo mathsize="0.800em">∈</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">[</mo><mrow><mn mathsize="0.800em">2</mn><mo mathsize="0.800em">,</mo><mi mathsize="0.800em">p</mi></mrow><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\delta y_{i}=\frac{\partial y_{i}}{\partial w_{i}}v_{i}+\frac{\partial y_{i}}{\partial y_{i-1}}\delta y_{i-1},\;\;\text{for }i\in[2,p]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p5.2" class="ltx_p">until the final scalar perturbation in the loss <math id="S2.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math> is computed.
Here, the <em id="S2.p5.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Jacobian</em> term <math id="S2.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{\partial y_{i}}{\partial w_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\partial y_{i}}{\partial w_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math> reflects sensitivity to weight changes, and the perturbation <em id="S2.p5.2.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">vector</em> is <math id="S2.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{i}\in\boldsymbol{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>𝒗</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{i}\in\boldsymbol{v}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S2.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒗</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{v}</annotation></semantics></math> is typically sampled from <math id="S2.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}(0,I_{d})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}(0,I_{d})</annotation></semantics></math>.
The scalar <math id="S2.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math> is referred to as the <span id="S2.p5.2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">jvp</span>. Weight gradients (also called forward gradients) are computed as <math id="S2.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{\partial\mathcal{L}}{\partial w_{i}}=\texttt{jvp}\cdot v_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">jvp</mtext><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\partial\mathcal{L}}{\partial w_{i}}=\texttt{jvp}\cdot v_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>.
In contrast to BP, which propagates <math id="S2.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{\partial\mathcal{L}}{\partial y_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\partial\mathcal{L}}{\partial y_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math> backward (<math id="S2.p5.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> from <math id="S2.p5.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S2.p5.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>), <span id="S2.p5.2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">FmAD</span> propagates <math id="S2.p5.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{\partial y_{i}}{\partial w_{j}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\partial y_{i}}{\partial w_{j}}</annotation></semantics></math> forward (<math id="S2.p5.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> from <math id="S2.p5.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S2.p5.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>, for all <math id="S2.p5.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Zero-order (ZO) Finite Differences.</span>
ZO optimization estimates gradients using only function <math id="S2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> evaluations, with no first-order derivative information required.
These methods, including finite differences <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>, <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>, perturb the weights and approximate gradients through changes in the loss values of the perturbed function evaluations.
Given a perturbation direction <math id="S2.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{v}\sim\mathcal{N}(0,I_{d})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒗</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{v}\sim\mathcal{N}(0,I_{d})</annotation></semantics></math>, the gradient with respect to <math id="S2.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is approximated via:</p>
<table id="S2.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\frac{\partial\mathcal{L}}{\partial w_{i}}\approx\frac{\mathcal{L}(f(\boldsymbol{w}+\epsilon\boldsymbol{v},x),\hat{y})-\mathcal{L}(f(\boldsymbol{w}-\epsilon\boldsymbol{v},x),\hat{y})}{2\epsilon}\cdot v_{i}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mo mathsize="0.800em">∂</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.800em">ℒ</mi></mrow><mrow><mo mathsize="0.800em">∂</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">w</mi><mi mathsize="0.800em">i</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo mathsize="0.800em">≈</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.800em">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">(</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">(</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">𝒘</mi><mo mathsize="0.800em">+</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">ϵ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.800em">𝒗</mi></mrow></mrow><mo mathsize="0.800em">,</mo><mi mathsize="0.800em">x</mi><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo mathsize="0.800em">,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi mathsize="0.800em">y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo mathsize="0.800em">−</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.800em">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">(</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">(</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">𝒘</mi><mo mathsize="0.800em">−</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">ϵ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.800em">𝒗</mi></mrow></mrow><mo mathsize="0.800em">,</mo><mi mathsize="0.800em">x</mi><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo mathsize="0.800em">,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi mathsize="0.800em">y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mn mathsize="0.800em">2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.800em">ϵ</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0.222em" mathsize="0.800em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">v</mi><mi mathsize="0.800em">i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo mathsize="0.800em">,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\frac{\partial\mathcal{L}}{\partial w_{i}}\approx\frac{\mathcal{L}(f(\boldsymbol{w}+\epsilon\boldsymbol{v},x),\hat{y})-\mathcal{L}(f(\boldsymbol{w}-\epsilon\boldsymbol{v},x),\hat{y})}{2\epsilon}\cdot v_{i},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p6.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math> is a small step size.
This symmetric difference estimator requires two sequential forward passes per perturbation direction.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Theoretical Analysis</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We begin by analyzing the fundamental convergence, memory consumption, and computational characteristics of backpropagation (BP), forward-mode automatic differentiation (<span id="S3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">FmAD</span>), and zero-order (ZO) optimization.
While these methods have been studied in prior works <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>, <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>, <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>, <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite>, a unified theoretical framework comparing their convergence behavior, memory consumption, and computation costs is still lacking.
In this section, we place all three approaches within a common mathematical setting and derive bounds on their per-iteration computational complexity and convergence rates.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Our analysis reveals that, although <span id="S3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">FmAD</span> and ZO may offer memory savings, they suffer from critical limitations in high-dimensional regimes or when the number of perturbations is restricted, leading to slower convergence and higher overall computation cost.
In contrast, BP, especially when combined with activation checkpointing, exhibits more favorable scaling in both computation and convergence while maintaining competitive memory efficiency.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Below, we first present the convergence bounds on a non-convex function <math id="S3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>, for the three gradient computation approaches studied in this work — BP, <span id="S3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">FmAD</span> and ZO.
These bounds highlight how convergence behavior is affected by key parameters, including the trainable parameter dimensionality <math id="S3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> and the number of perturbations per iteration <math id="S3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>.
All notations used here follow the definitions in §<a href="#S2" title="2 Background ‣ The Cost of Avoiding Backpropagation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, and Appendix <a href="#A9" title="Appendix I Proofs of Convergence Bounds ‣ The Cost of Avoiding Backpropagation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">I</span></a> contains the complete proofs.
Appendix <a href="#A8" title="Appendix H Computational Complexity ‣ The Cost of Avoiding Backpropagation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">H</span></a> presents the detailed analysis of compute costs.
Here we highlight our main takeaways from the analysis.
Table <a href="#S3.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 3 Theoretical Analysis ‣ The Cost of Avoiding Backpropagation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> summarizes the key theoretical results.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.T1" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering" style="font-size:80%;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S3.T1.14" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:113%;">Table 1</span>: </span><span id="S3.T1.15" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:113%;">
Big <math id="S3.T1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}</annotation></semantics></math> bounds of gradient computation methods on
(a) Convergence error,
(b) Memory consumption, and
(c) Compute cost (per-iteration).
Let
<math id="S3.T1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> denote the memory required to store activations for a single layer,
<math id="S3.T1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{h}</annotation></semantics></math> the maximum per-layer activation memory,
<math id="S3.T1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> the total number of layers, and
<math id="S3.T1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> the number of trainable parameters.
While <span id="S3.T1.15.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">BP</span> with checkpointing retains the fast convergence of BP with additional memory savings; both <span id="S3.T1.15.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">FmAD</span> and ZO methods suffer from worse convergence and higher compute costs, with parallel variants further increasing memory consumption.
</span></figcaption>
<table id="S3.T1.16" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="S3.T1.16.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S3.T1.16.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt" style="padding-top:3.2pt;padding-bottom:3.2pt;"><span id="S3.T1.16.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">Method</span></th>
<th id="S3.T1.16.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt" style="padding-top:3.2pt;padding-bottom:3.2pt;"><span id="S3.T1.16.1.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">Convergence Error</span></th>
<th id="S3.T1.16.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt" style="padding-top:3.2pt;padding-bottom:3.2pt;"><span id="S3.T1.16.1.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">Memory</span></th>
<th id="S3.T1.16.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt" style="padding-top:3.2pt;padding-bottom:3.2pt;"><span id="S3.T1.16.1.4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">Compute</span></th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S3.T1.16.2" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S3.T1.16.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:3.2pt;padding-bottom:3.2pt;"><span id="S3.T1.16.2.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">BP</span></td>
<td id="S3.T1.16.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:3.2pt;padding-bottom:3.2pt;" rowspan="2"><span id="S3.T1.16.2.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">
<span id="S3.T1.16.2.2.1.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_middle">
<span id="S3.T1.16.2.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="S3.T1.16.2.2.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_left" style="padding-top:3.2pt;padding-bottom:3.2pt;"><math id="S3.T1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(1/T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(1/T)</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S3.T1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta\leq\frac{1}{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>≤</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>L</mi></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta\leq\frac{1}{L}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span>
</span></span></td>
<td id="S3.T1.16.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:3.2pt;padding-bottom:3.2pt;"><math id="S3.T1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(cD)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.800em">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">(</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.800em">D</mi></mrow><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(cD)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S3.T1.16.2.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:3.2pt;padding-bottom:3.2pt;"><math id="S3.T1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(d)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.800em">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">(</mo><mi mathsize="0.800em">d</mi><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(d)</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T1.16.3" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S3.T1.16.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:3.2pt;padding-bottom:3.2pt;"><span id="S3.T1.16.3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps" style="font-size:80%;">BP<span id="S3.T1.16.3.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright"> (with checkpointing)</span></span></td>
<td id="S3.T1.16.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:3.2pt;padding-bottom:3.2pt;"><math id="S3.T1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(c\sqrt{D})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.800em">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">(</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mi mathsize="0.800em">D</mi></msqrt></mrow><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(c\sqrt{D})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S3.T1.16.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:3.2pt;padding-bottom:3.2pt;"><math id="S3.T1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(d\log D)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.800em">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">(</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">d</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi mathsize="0.800em">D</mi></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(d\log D)</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T1.16.4" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S3.T1.16.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:3.2pt;padding-bottom:3.2pt;"><span id="S3.T1.16.4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps" style="font-size:80%;">FmAD<span id="S3.T1.16.4.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright"> (Parallel)</span></span></td>
<td id="S3.T1.16.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:3.2pt;padding-bottom:3.2pt;" rowspan="2"><span id="S3.T1.16.4.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">
<span id="S3.T1.16.4.2.1.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_middle">
<span id="S3.T1.16.4.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="S3.T1.16.4.2.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_left" style="padding-top:3.2pt;padding-bottom:3.2pt;"><math id="S3.T1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}\left(\frac{1}{T\left[1-\frac{L\eta}{2}\left(1+\frac{d+1}{n}\right)\right]}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></mfrac></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}\left(\frac{1}{T\left[1-\frac{L\eta}{2}\left(1+\frac{d+1}{n}\right)\right]}\right)</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S3.T1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta&lt;\frac{2}{L\left(1+\frac{d+1}{n}\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></mfrac></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta&lt;\frac{2}{L\left(1+\frac{d+1}{n}\right)}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span>
</span></span></td>
<td id="S3.T1.16.4.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:3.2pt;padding-bottom:3.2pt;"><math id="S3.T1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(nc_{h})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.800em">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">(</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">c</mi><mi mathsize="0.800em">h</mi></msub></mrow><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(nc_{h})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S3.T1.16.4.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:3.2pt;padding-bottom:3.2pt;"><math id="S3.T1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(nd)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.800em">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">(</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.800em">d</mi></mrow><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(nd)</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T1.16.5" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S3.T1.16.5.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:3.2pt;padding-bottom:3.2pt;"><span id="S3.T1.16.5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps" style="font-size:80%;">FmAD<span id="S3.T1.16.5.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright"> (Sequential)</span></span></td>
<td id="S3.T1.16.5.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:3.2pt;padding-bottom:3.2pt;"><math id="S3.T1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(c_{h})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.800em">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">(</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">c</mi><mi mathsize="0.800em">h</mi></msub><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(c_{h})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S3.T1.16.5.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:3.2pt;padding-bottom:3.2pt;"><math id="S3.T1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(nd)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.800em">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">(</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.800em">d</mi></mrow><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(nd)</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T1.16.6" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S3.T1.16.6.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:3.2pt;padding-bottom:3.2pt;"><span id="S3.T1.16.6.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">ZO (Parallel)</span></td>
<td id="S3.T1.16.6.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:3.2pt;padding-bottom:3.2pt;" rowspan="2"><span id="S3.T1.16.6.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">
<span id="S3.T1.16.6.2.1.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_middle">
<span id="S3.T1.16.6.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="S3.T1.16.6.2.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_left" style="padding-top:3.2pt;padding-bottom:3.2pt;"><math id="S3.T1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}\left(\frac{1}{T\left[1-\frac{L\eta}{2}\left(1+\frac{d+1}{n}\right)\right]}\right)+\frac{Ld\eta^{2}}{2n}\mathcal{O}(\epsilon^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></mfrac></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>η</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}\left(\frac{1}{T\left[1-\frac{L\eta}{2}\left(1+\frac{d+1}{n}\right)\right]}\right)+\frac{Ld\eta^{2}}{2n}\mathcal{O}(\epsilon^{2})</annotation></semantics></math></span></span>
<span id="S3.T1.16.6.2.1.1.2" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="S3.T1.16.6.2.1.1.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_left" style="padding-top:3.2pt;padding-bottom:3.2pt;">with <math id="S3.T1.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta&lt;\frac{2}{L\left(1+\frac{d+1}{n}\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></mfrac></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta&lt;\frac{2}{L\left(1+\frac{d+1}{n}\right)}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span>
</span></span></td>
<td id="S3.T1.16.6.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:3.2pt;padding-bottom:3.2pt;"><math id="S3.T1.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(nc_{h})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.800em">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">(</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">c</mi><mi mathsize="0.800em">h</mi></msub></mrow><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(nc_{h})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S3.T1.16.6.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:3.2pt;padding-bottom:3.2pt;"><math id="S3.T1.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(nd)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.800em">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">(</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.800em">d</mi></mrow><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(nd)</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T1.16.7" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S3.T1.16.7.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:3.2pt;padding-bottom:3.2pt;"><span id="S3.T1.16.7.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">ZO (Sequential)</span></td>
<td id="S3.T1.16.7.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:3.2pt;padding-bottom:3.2pt;"><math id="S3.T1.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(c_{h})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.800em">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">(</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">c</mi><mi mathsize="0.800em">h</mi></msub><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(c_{h})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S3.T1.16.7.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:3.2pt;padding-bottom:3.2pt;"><math id="S3.T1.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(nd)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.800em">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">(</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.800em">d</mi></mrow><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(nd)</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</figure>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 3.1</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Error Bound for Backpropagation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>, <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite>)<span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:\mathbb{R}^{d}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:\mathbb{R}^{d}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> be an <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>-smooth function.
Under gradient descent with step size <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0&lt;\eta\leq\frac{1}{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>η</mi><mo>≤</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>L</mi></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0&lt;\eta\leq\frac{1}{L}</annotation></semantics></math>, the following holds:</span></p>
<table id="S3.EGx3" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\min_{t\in[T]}||\nabla f(\boldsymbol{w}_{t})||^{2}\leq\frac{2L}{T}\left(f(\boldsymbol{w}_{1})-f(\boldsymbol{w}_{T})\right)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mi mathsize="0.800em">min</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">t</mi><mo mathsize="0.800em">∈</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">[</mo><mi mathsize="0.800em">T</mi><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">]</mo></mrow></mrow></munder><mo>⁡</mo><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em" stretchy="true">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mo mathsize="0.800em" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi mathsize="0.800em">f</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">(</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">𝒘</mi><mi mathsize="0.800em">t</mi></msub><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em" stretchy="true">‖</mo></mrow><mn mathsize="0.800em">2</mn></msup></mrow><mo mathsize="0.800em">≤</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mn mathsize="0.800em">2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.800em">L</mi></mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">T</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">(</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">𝒘</mi><mn mathsize="0.800em">1</mn></msub><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo mathsize="0.800em">−</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">(</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">𝒘</mi><mi mathsize="0.800em">T</mi></msub><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo mathsize="0.800em">,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\min_{t\in[T]}||\nabla f(\boldsymbol{w}_{t})||^{2}\leq\frac{2L}{T}\left(f(\boldsymbol{w}_{1})-f(\boldsymbol{w}_{T})\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> is the number of optimization steps.
This classic result guarantees an <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(1/T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(1/T)</annotation></semantics></math> convergence rate in gradient norm, which is optimal for first-order methods.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 3.2</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Error Bound for Zero-Order Optimization)<span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:\mathbb{R}^{d}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:\mathbb{R}^{d}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> be an <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>-smooth function.
Let the gradient estimator at step <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> be computed using <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> independent perturbations <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{v}_{i}\sim\mathcal{N}(0,I_{d})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{v}_{i}\sim\mathcal{N}(0,I_{d})</annotation></semantics></math> and step size <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>η</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta</annotation></semantics></math> as follows:</span></p>
<table id="S3.EGx4" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E4"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\hat{g}(\boldsymbol{w}_{t})=\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\left(\frac{f(\boldsymbol{w}_{t}+\epsilon\boldsymbol{v}_{i})-f(\boldsymbol{w}_{t}-\epsilon\boldsymbol{v}_{i})}{2\epsilon}\boldsymbol{v}_{i}\right)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi mathsize="0.800em">g</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">(</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">𝒘</mi><mi mathsize="0.800em">t</mi></msub><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo mathsize="0.800em">=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn mathsize="0.800em">1</mn><mi mathsize="0.800em">n</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em" movablelimits="false" stretchy="true">∑</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">i</mi><mo mathsize="0.800em">=</mo><mn mathsize="0.800em">1</mn></mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">n</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">𝒘</mi><mi mathsize="0.800em">t</mi></msub><mo mathsize="0.800em">+</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">ϵ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">𝒗</mi><mi mathsize="0.800em">i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo mathsize="0.800em">−</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">𝒘</mi><mi mathsize="0.800em">t</mi></msub><mo mathsize="0.800em">−</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">ϵ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">𝒗</mi><mi mathsize="0.800em">i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mn mathsize="0.800em">2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.800em">ϵ</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">𝒗</mi><mi mathsize="0.800em">i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" mathsize="0.800em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\hat{g}(\boldsymbol{w}_{t})=\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\left(\frac{f(\boldsymbol{w}_{t}+\epsilon\boldsymbol{v}_{i})-f(\boldsymbol{w}_{t}-\epsilon\boldsymbol{v}_{i})}{2\epsilon}\boldsymbol{v}_{i}\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Then, the expected convergence error is bounded by</span></p>
<table id="S3.EGx5" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E5"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\min_{t\in[T]}||\nabla f(\boldsymbol{w}_{t})||^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder><mi mathsize="0.800em">min</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">t</mi><mo mathsize="0.800em">∈</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">[</mo><mi mathsize="0.800em">T</mi><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">]</mo></mrow></mrow></munder><mo>⁡</mo><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em" stretchy="true">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mo mathsize="0.800em" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi mathsize="0.800em">f</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">(</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">𝒘</mi><mi mathsize="0.800em">t</mi></msub><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em" stretchy="true">‖</mo></mrow><mn mathsize="0.800em">2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\min_{t\in[T]}||\nabla f(\boldsymbol{w}_{t})||^{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\leq\frac{f(\boldsymbol{w}_{1})-f(\boldsymbol{w}_{T})}{\eta T\left[1-\frac{L\eta}{2}\left(1+\frac{d+1}{n}\right)\right]}+\frac{Ld\eta^{2}}{2n}\mathcal{O}(\epsilon^{2})," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo mathsize="0.800em">≤</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">(</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">𝒘</mi><mn mathsize="0.800em">1</mn></msub><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo mathsize="0.800em">−</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">(</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">𝒘</mi><mi mathsize="0.800em">T</mi></msub><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">η</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.800em">T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mn mathsize="0.800em">1</mn><mo mathsize="0.800em">−</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.800em">η</mi></mrow><mn mathsize="0.800em">2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mn mathsize="0.800em">1</mn><mo mathsize="0.800em">+</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">d</mi><mo mathsize="0.800em">+</mo><mn mathsize="0.800em">1</mn></mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">n</mi></mfrac></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo mathsize="0.800em">+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.800em">d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi mathsize="0.800em">η</mi><mn mathsize="0.800em">2</mn></msup></mrow><mrow><mn mathsize="0.800em">2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.800em">n</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.800em">𝒪</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">(</mo><msup><mi mathsize="0.800em">ϵ</mi><mn mathsize="0.800em">2</mn></msup><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo mathsize="0.800em">,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\leq\frac{f(\boldsymbol{w}_{1})-f(\boldsymbol{w}_{T})}{\eta T\left[1-\frac{L\eta}{2}\left(1+\frac{d+1}{n}\right)\right]}+\frac{Ld\eta^{2}}{2n}\mathcal{O}(\epsilon^{2}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">provided that <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta&lt;\frac{2}{L(1+\frac{d+1}{n})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></mfrac></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta&lt;\frac{2}{L(1+\frac{d+1}{n})}</annotation></semantics></math>.
This bound shows that performance degrades with increasing dimensionality <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> or decreasing number of perturbations <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, resulting in slower convergence and higher error.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 3.3</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Error Bound for Forward-Mode AD)<span id="S3.Thmtheorem3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:\mathbb{R}^{d}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:\mathbb{R}^{d}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> be an <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>-smooth function.
Let the forward-mode AD gradient estimator at step <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> use <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> perturbations <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{v}_{i}\sim\mathcal{N}(0,I_{d})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{v}_{i}\sim\mathcal{N}(0,I_{d})</annotation></semantics></math>) and define:</span></p>
<table id="S3.EGx6" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E6"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E6.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\hat{g}(\boldsymbol{w}_{t})=\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\left(\left(\boldsymbol{v}_{i}^{\top}\nabla f(\boldsymbol{w}_{t})\right)\boldsymbol{v}_{i}\right)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi mathsize="0.800em">g</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">(</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">𝒘</mi><mi mathsize="0.800em">t</mi></msub><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">)</mo></mrow><mo mathsize="0.800em">=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn mathsize="0.800em">1</mn><mi mathsize="0.800em">n</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em" movablelimits="false" stretchy="true">∑</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">i</mi><mo mathsize="0.800em">=</mo><mn mathsize="0.800em">1</mn></mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">n</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.800em">𝒗</mi><mi mathsize="0.800em">i</mi><mo mathsize="0.800em">⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em" mathsize="0.800em" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi mathsize="0.800em">f</mi><mrow><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">(</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">𝒘</mi><mi mathsize="0.800em">t</mi></msub><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">)</mo></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">𝒗</mi><mi mathsize="0.800em">i</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" mathsize="0.800em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\hat{g}(\boldsymbol{w}_{t})=\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\left(\left(\boldsymbol{v}_{i}^{\top}\nabla f(\boldsymbol{w}_{t})\right)\boldsymbol{v}_{i}\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Then, the expected convergence error is bounded by</span></p>
<table id="S3.EGx7" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E7"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\min_{t\in[T]}||\nabla f(\boldsymbol{w}_{t})||^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder><mi mathsize="0.800em">min</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">t</mi><mo mathsize="0.800em">∈</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">[</mo><mi mathsize="0.800em">T</mi><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">]</mo></mrow></mrow></munder><mo>⁡</mo><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em" stretchy="true">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mo mathsize="0.800em" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi mathsize="0.800em">f</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">(</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">𝒘</mi><mi mathsize="0.800em">t</mi></msub><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em" stretchy="true">‖</mo></mrow><mn mathsize="0.800em">2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\min_{t\in[T]}||\nabla f(\boldsymbol{w}_{t})||^{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\leq\frac{f(\boldsymbol{w}_{1})-f(\boldsymbol{w}_{T})}{\eta T\left[1-\frac{L\eta}{2}\left(1+\frac{d+1}{n}\right)\right]}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo mathsize="0.800em">≤</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">(</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">𝒘</mi><mn mathsize="0.800em">1</mn></msub><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo mathsize="0.800em">−</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">(</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">𝒘</mi><mi mathsize="0.800em">T</mi></msub><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">η</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.800em">T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mn mathsize="0.800em">1</mn><mo mathsize="0.800em">−</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.800em">η</mi></mrow><mn mathsize="0.800em">2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mn mathsize="0.800em">1</mn><mo mathsize="0.800em">+</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">d</mi><mo mathsize="0.800em">+</mo><mn mathsize="0.800em">1</mn></mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">n</mi></mfrac></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo lspace="0em" mathsize="0.800em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\leq\frac{f(\boldsymbol{w}_{1})-f(\boldsymbol{w}_{T})}{\eta T\left[1-\frac{L\eta}{2}\left(1+\frac{d+1}{n}\right)\right]}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">with convergence requiring <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta&lt;\frac{2}{L(1+\frac{d+1}{n})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></mfrac></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta&lt;\frac{2}{L(1+\frac{d+1}{n})}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Similar to ZO, the convergence rate deteriorates as
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> increases or
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> decreases, yielding slower convergence.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">We summarize key theoretical insights comparing BP, <span id="S3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">FmAD</span>, and ZO across three axes: convergence error, convergence time, and memory-compute efficiency.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Finding 1: <span id="S3.p5.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">FmAD</span> and ZO incur higher error than BP due to approximation and discretization effects.</span>
In contrast to BP, which computes exact gradients (modulo floating-point precision), <span id="S3.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">FmAD</span> and ZO rely on approximations that introduce estimation bias and noise.
ZO methods, in particular, suffer from discretization errors stemming from finite-difference estimation.
The convergence bound includes an additive term
<math id="S3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{Ld\eta^{2}}{2n}\mathcal{O}(\epsilon^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>η</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{Ld\eta^{2}}{2n}\mathcal{O}(\epsilon^{2})</annotation></semantics></math>
which captures variance and truncation effects introduced by perturbations.
<span id="S3.p5.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">FmAD</span> avoids this additional error term but retains the same sensitivity to <math id="S3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, indicating its convergence is still hampered in large models or under low perturbation budgets due to the effects of random perturbations.
§<a href="#S4.SS2" title="4.2 Comparison on Accuracy ‣ 4 Empirical Evaluation ‣ The Cost of Avoiding Backpropagation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a> empirically demonstrates that ZO suffers greater accuracy degradation than <span id="S3.p5.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">FmAD</span> due to discretization error, and that both ZO and <span id="S3.p5.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">FmAD</span> achieve lower accuracy than BP because of additional learning rate constraints, which are detailed in Appendix <a href="#A3" title="Appendix C Baselines and Hyperparameters ‣ The Cost of Avoiding Backpropagation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Finding 2: Convergence speed of <span id="S3.p6.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">FmAD</span> and ZO degrades with higher dimensionality or fewer perturbations.</span>
While BP achieves an optimal convergence rate of
<math id="S3.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(1/T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(1/T)</annotation></semantics></math> under standard smoothness and learning rate assumptions, both <span id="S3.p6.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">FmAD</span> and ZO introduce additional fundamental constraints. In particular, they require a more conservative step size of <math id="S3.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta&lt;\frac{2}{L(1+\frac{d+1}{n})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></mfrac></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta&lt;\frac{2}{L(1+\frac{d+1}{n})}</annotation></semantics></math>
which becomes increasingly restrictive as the parameter dimensionality <math id="S3.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> increases or the number of perturbations
<math id="S3.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> decreases. This directly slows convergence, especially in high-dimensional models or when perturbation sampling is limited due to additional compute or memory cost, highlighting a core scalability bottleneck in both methods.
§<a href="#S4.SS3" title="4.3 Comparison on Wallclock Convergence Time ‣ 4 Empirical Evaluation ‣ The Cost of Avoiding Backpropagation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a> supports this finding by showing that <span id="S3.p6.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">FmAD</span> and ZO converge more slowly and reach lower accuracy compared to BP.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Finding 3: Both <span id="S3.p7.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">FmAD</span> and ZO have higher compute cost per iteration as the number of perturbations increase, and must trade memory for convergence speed. </span>
Table <a href="#S3.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 3 Theoretical Analysis ‣ The Cost of Avoiding Backpropagation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> compares the theoretical memory and compute complexity of the three methods.
BP computes gradients in <math id="S3.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(d)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(d)</annotation></semantics></math> time per iteration.
<span id="S3.p7.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">FmAD</span> and ZO, by contrast, if performing <math id="S3.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> gradient estimations per iteration, yield a per-iteration complexity of <math id="S3.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(nd)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(nd)</annotation></semantics></math>.
When using checkpointing, BP incurs a modest recomputation overhead of <math id="S3.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log D</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> is the network depth.
In terms of memory, BP requires <math id="S3.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(cD)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(cD)</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S3.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> is the cost of one layer’s activation.
BP with checkpointing reduces this memory cost by discarding some of the activations, incurring <math id="S3.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(c\sqrt{D})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mi>D</mi></msqrt></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(c\sqrt{D})</annotation></semantics></math>.
<span id="S3.p7.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">FmAD</span>/ZO in parallel mode, where multiple perturbations are evaluated in parallel, require <math id="S3.p7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(nc_{h})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>h</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(nc_{h})</annotation></semantics></math> memory, where <math id="S3.p7.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{h}</annotation></semantics></math> is the memory cost of the largest activation, as each perturbed forward pass consumes <math id="S3.p7.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{h}</annotation></semantics></math>.
<span id="S3.p7.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">FmAD</span>/ZO in sequential mode reduces memory to <math id="S3.p7.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(c_{h})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(c_{h})</annotation></semantics></math> but incur higher wallclock runtime.
Thus, faster convergence with <span id="S3.p7.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">FmAD</span> and ZO requires higher memory usage (via more parallel perturbations), while memory-efficient sequential variants suffer longer training times.
§<a href="#S4.SS4" title="4.4 Comparison on Memory Consumption ‣ 4 Empirical Evaluation ‣ The Cost of Avoiding Backpropagation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.4</span></a> corroborates these memory bounds and shows a breakdown of the memory consumption.
§<a href="#S4.SS5" title="4.5 Comparison on Computation Cost ‣ 4 Empirical Evaluation ‣ The Cost of Avoiding Backpropagation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.5</span></a> empirically validates the computation cost.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">A note on non-differentiable and black-box settings.</span>
While it’s claimed that ZO has utility in settings with non-differentiable objectives <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>, <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite> or limited model access <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>, <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite>, its applicability to large-scale model training is fundamentally constrained.
In true black-box scenarios, it is often infeasible to perturb weights or query the loss values, making ZO methods impractical.
In contrast, first-order methods such as BP and <span id="S3.p8.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">FmAD</span> require access to model internals and automatic differentiation support – challenges that are largely engineering in nature and increasingly well-supported by modern frameworks.
As such, the growing trend <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>, <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite> of applying ZO to train LLMs is misguided:
the computational cost and degraded convergence significantly outweigh the memory gains.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Empirical Evaluation</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we corroborate our theoretical findings with empirical experiments that systematically compare the variants of backpropagation (BP), forward-mode automatic differentiation (<span id="S4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">FmAD</span>), and zero-order (ZO) optimization.
We evaluate these methods across multiple axes, including
(a) accuracy,
(b) wallclock convergence time,
(c) memory consumption, and
(d) computation cost.
For each of these dimensions, we also examine how different variance reduction strategies affect performance.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Experimental Settings</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Datasets.</span>
We evaluate gradient computation methods across a diverse set of <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">5 text-based tasks</span> and <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">2 vision-based tasks</span>.
The 5 text-based tasks are
(a) Gsm8K (text generation on math problems/next-word prediction) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite>,
(b) MMLU (multiple-choice question-answering covering various domains of knowledge) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite>,
(c) AGNews (4-class news article text classification task) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite>,
(d) BoolQ (boolean question-answering) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite>, and
(e) MultiRC (closed-book question-answering) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>]</cite>.
The 2 vision-based tasks are both based on visual question-answering:
(a) VQAv2 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>]</cite>, and
(b) TextVQA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>]</cite>.
Appendix <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Datasets ‣ The Cost of Avoiding Backpropagation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a> describes the datasets in detail, including the train/test splits.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Models.</span>
Our evaluation uses 5 models with a varying number of total parameters (listed in parentheses).
For text-based tasks, on the billion-parameters scale, we use <span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Llama 3.1</span> (8B) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>]</cite>
and <span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">OPT</span> (1.3B, 6.7B, 13B) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>]</cite>.
Additionally, we include medium-sized language models
<span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Bert</span> (110M, 340M) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>]</cite>
and <span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">RoBERTa</span> (125M, 355M) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>]</cite> to analyze performance variations across model sizes.
For vision-based tasks, we use
<span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Qwen 2 VL</span> (7B) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>]</cite>. To finetune these models, we use <span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">QLoRA</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>]</cite>, where low-rank adapters are trainable while the rest of the weights are frozen and quantized to 4 bits.
By default, we set the <span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">LoRA</span> rank <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=1</annotation></semantics></math> and scale <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=1</annotation></semantics></math> to minimize the number of trainable parameters for <span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">FmAD</span> and ZO.
Appendix <a href="#A6.SS4" title="F.4 Reducing Trainable Parameter Count ‣ Appendix F Additional Results ‣ The Cost of Avoiding Backpropagation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">F.4</span></a> reports results on higher <span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">LoRA</span> ranks.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Methods for Comparison.</span>
We categorize the 16 gradient computation methods our evaluation compares into three groups:
(a) <span id="S4.SS1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Backpropagation Methods:</span>
<span id="S4.SS1.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">BP-Vanilla</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite> (the standard implementation that stores all intermediate activations),
<span id="S4.SS1.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">BP-Checkpointing</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite> (reduces peak memory consumption by storing only a subset of activations and recomputing the rest during the backward pass),
and <span id="S4.SS1.p3.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">BP-Accumulate</span> (uses gradient accumulation).
(b) <span id="S4.SS1.p3.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Zero-order Methods</span>:
<span id="S4.SS1.p3.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ZO-Vanilla</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite> (use a single perturbation to estimate gradients as in Equation <a href="#S2.E2" title="In 2 Background ‣ The Cost of Avoiding Backpropagation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>),
<span id="S4.SS1.p3.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">MeZO</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite> (incorporates a prompt-finetuning approach to convert classification tasks into next-word prediction with a constrained vocabulary),
<span id="S4.SS1.p3.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ZO-Accumulate</span> (applies gradient accumulation to reduce noise in gradient estimates),
<span id="S4.SS1.p3.1.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ZO-Multiple</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite> (averages gradient estimates from multiple perturbations per iteration to improve estimate stability),
<span id="S4.SS1.p3.1.11" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ZO-Adaptive</span> (adaptively selects perturbation directions based on prior gradients),
<span id="S4.SS1.p3.1.12" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ZO-SVRG</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite> (applies stochastic variance reduction to correct noisy gradients),
and <span id="S4.SS1.p3.1.13" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ZO-Sparse</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite> (only updates top-1% parameters each iteration).
(c) <span id="S4.SS1.p3.1.14" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Forward-mode AD Methods:</span>
<span id="S4.SS1.p3.1.15" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">FmAD-Vanilla</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>,
<span id="S4.SS1.p3.1.16" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">FmAD-Accumulate</span>,
<span id="S4.SS1.p3.1.17" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">FmAD-Multiple</span>,
<span id="S4.SS1.p3.1.18" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">FmAD-Adaptive</span>,
<span id="S4.SS1.p3.1.19" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">FmAD-SVRG</span>, and
<span id="S4.SS1.p3.1.20" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">FmAD-Sparse</span>.
The <span id="S4.SS1.p3.1.21" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">-Vanilla</span> suffix denotes the original implementation according to Equation <a href="#S3.E6" title="In Theorem 3.3 (Error Bound for Forward-Mode AD). ‣ 3 Theoretical Analysis ‣ The Cost of Avoiding Backpropagation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>, while the other variants mirror the corresponding ZO method in (b), adapting similar strategies for the forward-mode setting.
Appendix <a href="#A3" title="Appendix C Baselines and Hyperparameters ‣ The Cost of Avoiding Backpropagation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a> describes these methods and their hyperparameters in detail.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Metrics.</span>
We evaluate the efficiency of the gradient computation methods using four metrics.
(a) <span id="S4.SS1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Accuracy</span> at test-time assesses the efficacy of the learned models.
(b) <span id="S4.SS1.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Wallclock convergence time</span> (in minutes) measures the time each approach takes to achieve stable-state accuracy.
(c) <span id="S4.SS1.p4.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Peak memory consumption</span> (in GBs) quantifies the maximum memory consumed during training.
(d) <span id="S4.SS1.p4.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Computation cost</span> for each iteration and until convergence, in Tera Floating-Point Operations per Second (TFLOPs).
Additionally, in our failure mode analysis, for ZO and <span id="S4.SS1.p4.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">FmAD</span> approaches, Appendix <a href="#A6.SS5" title="F.5 Failure Mode Analysis ‣ Appendix F Additional Results ‣ The Cost of Avoiding Backpropagation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">F.5</span></a> reports statistics, such as the mean of effective gradient norms and <span id="S4.SS1.p4.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">jvp</span> values across iterations, capturing the instability of estimated gradients and its impact on optimization dynamics.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Libraries and Hardware.</span>
Our codebase is built using PyTorch <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite>. Quantization uses AutoGPTQ <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>]</cite>.
We conducted all experiments involving billion-scale models across ZO and <span id="S4.SS1.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">FmAD</span> variants on a single Nvidia L40 GPU (48GB RAM).
For experiments on OPT (13B) model, we used one Nvidia A100 (80GB RAM).
For <span id="S4.SS1.p5.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">BERT</span> and <span id="S4.SS1.p5.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">RoBERTa</span> models, we used an Nvidia 2080ti (11GB RAM).
We repeated each experiment three times with random seeds set to 0, 1, and 2 to ensure consistency and robustness.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Comparison on Accuracy</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Accuracy is the primary metric of interest since any gradient computation method that reduces memory consumption or computational cost is of little practical value if it cannot match the predictive performance of BP.
Table <a href="#S4.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 4.2 Comparison on Accuracy ‣ 4 Empirical Evaluation ‣ The Cost of Avoiding Backpropagation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> presents accuracy results and Appendix <a href="#A6.SS1" title="F.1 Experimental Variance and Loss Curves ‣ Appendix F Additional Results ‣ The Cost of Avoiding Backpropagation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">F.1</span></a> shows
variance across 3 executions.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.T2" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering" style="font-size:80%;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S4.T2.24" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:113%;">Table 2</span>: </span><span id="S4.T2.25" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:113%;">
Accuracies (higher is better) of BP, <span id="S4.T2.25.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">FmAD</span>, and ZO variants across models and datasets.
Subscripts show accuracy gaps from <span id="S4.T2.25.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">BP-Vanilla/Chkpt</span> (<span id="S4.T2.25.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Chkpt</span> = <span id="S4.T2.25.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Checkpointing</span>).
While BP remains the most accurate, <span id="S4.T2.25.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">FmAD</span> and ZO variants like <span id="S4.T2.25.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">-Accumulate</span> and <span id="S4.T2.25.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">-Multiple</span> offer notable gains over their <span id="S4.T2.25.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">-Vanilla</span> forms but still lag behind BP, especially on generation tasks like GSM8K.
Variance across runs is reported in Appendix <a href="#A6.SS1" title="F.1 Experimental Variance and Loss Curves ‣ Appendix F Additional Results ‣ The Cost of Avoiding Backpropagation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">F.1</span></a>.
